      "source": [
        "### A.2 What the town pays for\n",
        "From the dataset sheet and the target metric sheet:\n",
        "\n",
        "* everybody catches the flu (100% infection, winter indoor parties);\n",
        "* a vaccine posted to a home costs **$300**, and is wasted if the person does not want it;\n",
        "* anyone who **wants** a vaccine but is not sent one must be paid **$300 in cash** (they stay unvaccinated);\n",
        "* a flu hospitalisation costs **$18,500**; the chance of one depends on the person and on whether they were vaccinated.\n",
        "\n",
        "So for each resident there are four possible outcomes:\n",
        "\n",
        "| | resident would take it | resident would refuse it |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| **we post a vaccine** | $300 + vaccinated risk × $18,500 | $300 wasted + unvaccinated risk × $18,500 |\n",
        "| **we do not post one** | $300 cash + unvaccinated risk × $18,500 | unvaccinated risk × $18,500 |\n",
        "\n",
        "The $300 is paid in three of the four cells. The only cell where posting changes the hospital bill is the top-left one."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "4f87f461",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4f87f461"
      },
      "source": [
        "### A.3 Turning a probability into a decision\n",
        "Let `q` be the model's probability that a resident takes the vaccine, `r_u` and `r_v` their hospitalisation risk without and with the vaccine, `V = 300` and `H = 18,500`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Posting a vaccine instead of not posting one:\n",
        "\n",
        "* **expected extra spend** = `(1 − q) · V` (the dose is wasted when they refuse; when they accept we would have paid the cash anyway);\n",
        "* **expected saving** = `q · (r_u − r_v) · H` (a hospital stay avoided, only if they accept).\n",
        "\n",
        "Post the vaccine when the saving is larger than the extra spend:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$q \\cdot (r_u - r_v)\\,H \\;>\\; (1-q)\\,V \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad q \\;>\\; \\frac{V}{V + (r_u - r_v)\\,H}$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cut-off is **different for every resident**. Frail, older residents are worth a dose even when we are unsure they will use it; for a healthy 25-year-old the dose has to be almost certain to be used."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "2bb338a9",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2bb338a9"
      },
      "source": [
        "### A.4 Hospitalisation risk (generative-AI research)\n",
        "The sheet asks us to estimate hospitalisation probabilities with generative AI, asking more than one AI or more than one person. Three models were each asked the same question separately (prompt below), without seeing each other's answers or any suggested values, and the code uses the plain average of their answers.\n",
        "\n",
        "> *Everyone in a town is infected with seasonal flu (H3N2). For adults aged 18-34, 35-44, 45-54, 55-64 and 65+ with no chronic condition, what is P(hospitalised | infected, unvaccinated), per infection, not per symptomatic case? What fraction of hospitalisations does the vaccine prevent? By what factor does each chronic condition multiply the risk (compounding)?*\n",
        "\n",
        "| Asked | 18-34 | 35-44 | 45-54 | 55-64 | 65+ | vaccine cuts risk by | each chronic condition multiplies risk by |\n",
        "|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n",
        "| Claude Sonnet | 0.0015 | 0.002 | 0.004 | 0.007 | 0.022 | 40% | 2.0 |\n",
        "| Claude Haiku | 0.0002 | 0.0005 | 0.001 | 0.003 | 0.008 | 40% | 2.0 |\n",
        "| Claude Fable | 0.0012 | 0.0018 | 0.0028 | 0.0045 | 0.025 | 35% | 2.0 |\n",
        "| **Average (used)** | **0.0010** | **0.0014** | **0.0026** | **0.0048** | **0.0183** | **38.3%** | **2.0** |\n",
        "\n",
        "What the answers had in common: all three started from CDC seasonal-flu burden estimates (hospitalisations per symptomatic illness), scaled them down to *per infection* because many infections are mild or silent, and scaled down again for people with no chronic condition. They agree closely on the vaccine effect (35-40%, lower in H3N2 seasons) and the chronic multiplier (2.0). They disagree most on the absolute risk for younger adults (up to about 7 times), and all three said those values could be out by a factor of 2-3. That disagreement is why Part E re-scores everything from 0.4 to 2 times these risks.\n",
        "\n",
        "*If team members ask other AIs (e.g. Gemini, ChatGPT), add a row and update the average.*"
      ]
    },
