        "### B.12 What the exploration decided\n",
        "| Finding (cells above) | Decision |\n",
        "|---|---|\n",
        "| Uptake is 53.6%, close to 50/50 (B.2) | stratified folds; no resampling or class weights needed |\n",
        "| All 29 columns fall inside the sheet's ranges; no duplicate ids or rows; no train/test overlap (B.1) | no rows dropped, no capping |\n",
        "| 98.9% of missing employment sectors belong to people not employed; uptake with insurance missing is 46%, between insured (63%) and uninsured (26%) (B.2) | keep \"missing\" as its own level or flag, never fill with a guess (tested in C.3) |\n",
        "| Questions are skipped in blocks (e.g. the three opinion scales, correlation 0.87-0.93; education, marital and employment status, 0.86-0.90), and people with 5+ gaps take the jab less (41% vs 55%) (B.9) | missing-value flags carry information; tree models see NaN directly |\n",
        "| Perceived risk (V = 0.40), belief the jab works (0.39) and a doctor's recommendation (0.35) are the strongest signals; most behaviours are below 0.10 (B.8) | an engineered `doctor_and_belief` feature, and a `precautions` count added next to the seven weak flags so their combined signal is easier to use |\n",
        "| The opinion scales put \"Don't know\" at 3, in the middle (B.4) | test one-hot encoding of the opinion scales against treating them as numbers (C.3) |\n",
        "| A doctor's recommendation lifts uptake by 27 points for the 65+ but by 31-40 points for younger groups (B.10) | compare linear models with tree models, which can learn interactions |\n",
        "| The 65+ are 24.6% of residents but carry 73% of the expected hospital bill (B.6) | personal cut-off in the decision rule (A.3) |\n",
        "| A model cannot tell training rows from test rows (AUC 0.49); the largest single difference is 0.03 (B.11) | no shift: cross-validated bills are a fair guide to the test set |"
