        "id": "050147da"
      },
      "source": [
        "The next cell prints how much one accepted dose saves in expected hospital cost for different residents, against the $300 it costs. Where the saving is far below $300, a dose is only worth posting when the resident is both very likely to use it and at real risk, which is why the personal cut-off can post far fewer doses than the 0.5 rule. Accuracy can go down while the bill goes down too, which is the clearest evidence that accuracy is the wrong yardstick for this task."
      ]
    },
