        "## Part F. Delivery\n",
        "### F.1 The five submission files\n",
        "The brief asks for five results that are **all different**, ordered best to worst by the task metric. Models are taken in league-table order (cheapest cross-validated bill first). A model is skipped if its out-of-fold decisions agree with an already chosen model for more than 99% of residents, because it would effectively be the same submission twice.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Tie-break.** The lecturers will score our files with their own hospitalisation figures, not ours. So when two neighbouring models are tied within the noise (the cheaper one wins fewer than 15 of the 20 paired folds), the one with the better average rank across the 18 scenarios in Part E goes first. A clear win at our best estimate always keeps its place.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each chosen model is refitted on all 4,756 training residents. Its test probabilities go through the personal cut-off, and the result is saved as `challenge_submission_group_<GROUP_ID>_order_<k>.csv`, where order 1 is the lowest expected bill. The `seasonal_vaccine` column holds **our decision** (1.0 = post a vaccine, 0.0 = do not), in the same format as the example file. Every file is checked against the example before it is written."
      ]
    },
