      "source": [
        "**Reading the XAI critically (check against the outputs above and rewrite in the team's words):**\n",
        "* Compare the permutation heatmap across models: if every model relies on the same few survey answers, the result is a property of the data, not of one algorithm.\n",
        "* Permutation importance (dollars) and SHAP (probability) answer different questions. A feature can shift `q` a lot (high SHAP) yet barely change the bill if it mostly moves low-risk residents who are below their cut-off either way.\n",
        "* `age_group` and `number_chronic_med_conditions` also enter the bill through the cut-off, which permutation does not shuffle; their dollar importance here only reflects their role in predicting uptake.\n",
        "* Permutation importance understates features that are correlated with others (for example the opinion scales), because the model can lean on the partner when one is shuffled.\n",
        "* Partial dependence averages over everyone and hides interactions; SHAP's spread of dots shows where the effect differs between residents."
      ]
    },
